subproblems.natural_proof:
Source. This is Step 10 of the parent proof (M. G. Dall'Alba, The Besicovitch-Federer Projection Theorem, proof of Theorem 5.1), combining the siblings cone_density_positive (parent Step 5), cone_density_infinite (Step 6), infinite_fibres (Step 7) and cone_trichotomy (Steps 8–9). The hypothesis (Eil) is the exact frozen statement of the root-level sibling eilenberg_upper_integral_inequality; it is only passed on to infinite_fibres.

Setting. Assume (Eil); let n ≥ 1, ℓ ≥ 1, N := n + ℓ, and A ⊆ ℝ^N compact, purely n-unrectifiable, with Hⁿ(A) < ∞ (Hⁿ := μHE[n]). Goal: for Lebesgue-a.e. v ∈ (ℝ^N)ⁿ, if v is linearly independent then Hⁿ(frameProjection v '' A) = 0.

1. Scales and conditions. For i ∈ ℕ let δ_i := 1/(i+1) > 0. For a linear p : ℝ^N → ℝ^N and a ∈ ℝ^N write X_p(a, r, s) := {x : |p(x − a)| < s|x − a|} ∩ B(a, r) and
 Φ0_i(a, p): ∀c > 0 ∃ε > 0 ∀s ∈ (0, ε) ∀r ∈ (0, δ_i): Hⁿ(A ∩ X_p(a, r, s)) ≤ c(rs)ⁿ;
 Φ∞_i(a, p): ∀K ∈ ℝ ∀ε > 0 ∃s ∈ (0, ε) ∃r ∈ (0, δ_i): K(rs)ⁿ ≤ Hⁿ(A ∩ X_p(a, r, s));
 Ax_i(a, p): ∃b ∈ A with b ≠ a, |b − a| < δ_i and p(b − a) = 0.
 (These are verbatim the conditions in the statements of the siblings, with δ := δ_i.)

2. Good frames. For each i, cone_trichotomy (applied with n, ℓ ≥ 1, the compact set A with Hⁿ(A) < ∞, and δ := δ_i) gives: for Lebesgue-a.e. v, if v is linearly independent then Hⁿ(G_i(v)) = 0, where G_i(v) := {a ∈ A : not (Φ0_i ∨ Φ∞_i ∨ Ax_i)(a, frameProjection v)}. A countable intersection of full-measure sets has full measure (Mathlib ae_all_iff), so for Lebesgue-a.e. v: for every i, if v is linearly independent then Hⁿ(G_i(v)) = 0. Fix such a v that is linearly independent; it suffices to show Hⁿ(frameProjection v '' A) = 0.

3. The plane of v. Let W := span{v_1, …, v_n}. Since v is linearly independent, finrank W = n (Mathlib finrank_span_eq_card). By the definition of frameProjection, P := frameProjection v = W.starProjection. P is linear with operator norm ≤ 1 (Submodule.starProjection_norm_le), hence 1-Lipschitz, so Hⁿ(P(S)) ≤ Hⁿ(S) for every S ⊆ ℝ^N (Lipschitz image bound for Hausdorff measure; SimonGMT Chapter02.euclideanHausdorffMeasure_image_le_of_lipschitzOn with constant 1).

4. Exceptional sets. For i ∈ ℕ put G_i := G_i(v), A1_i := {a ∈ A : Φ0_i(a, P)}, A2_i := {a ∈ A : Φ∞_i(a, P)}, and A3 := {a ∈ A : {b ∈ A : Pb = Pa} is infinite}. Then:
 – Hⁿ(G_i) = 0 by 2;
 – Hⁿ(A1_i) = 0 by cone_density_positive (n ≥ 1, finrank W = n, A purely n-unrectifiable, δ_i > 0);
 – Hⁿ(P(A2_i)) = 0 by cone_density_infinite (n ≥ 1, finrank W = n, A compact with Hⁿ(A) < ∞, δ_i > 0);
 – Hⁿ(P(A3)) = 0 by infinite_fibres (applied to (Eil), n, ℓ, W with finrank W = n, and A with Hⁿ(A) < ∞).

5. Covering. Claim: A ⊆ ⋃_i (G_i ∪ A1_i) ∪ ⋃_i A2_i ∪ A3. Let a ∈ A lie in no G_i, A1_i, A2_i. For each i: a ∉ G_i gives (Φ0_i ∨ Φ∞_i ∨ Ax_i)(a, P); a ∉ A1_i and a ∉ A2_i exclude the first two alternatives, so Ax_i(a, P) holds: there is b_i ∈ A with b_i ≠ a, |b_i − a| < 1/(i+1) and P(b_i − a) = 0, i.e. Pb_i = Pa. Suppose S := {b ∈ A : Pb = Pa} were finite. Then S ∖ {a} is finite, hence closed, and does not contain a, so there is ε > 0 with B(a, ε) ∩ (S ∖ {a}) = ∅. Choose i with 1/(i+1) < ε; then b_i ∈ (S ∖ {a}) ∩ B(a, ε), a contradiction. Hence S is infinite and a ∈ A3.

6. Conclusion. By 5, P(A) ⊆ P(⋃_i (G_i ∪ A1_i)) ∪ ⋃_i P(A2_i) ∪ P(A3). By 3 and 4, Hⁿ(P(⋃_i (G_i ∪ A1_i))) ≤ Hⁿ(⋃_i (G_i ∪ A1_i)) ≤ Σ_i (Hⁿ(G_i) + Hⁿ(A1_i)) = 0, and Hⁿ(P(A2_i)) = 0 = Hⁿ(P(A3)). By countable subadditivity Hⁿ(P(A)) = 0, i.e. μHE[n] (frameProjection v '' A) = 0. Since this holds for Lebesgue-a.e. v whenever v is linearly independent, ForAlmostAllProjections (n + ℓ) n (fun p ↦ μHE[n] (p '' A) = 0). ∎
